Below is a minimal, self‑contained example showing how you would create and configure an **Elasticsearch** `ObjectParser<ParsedDocument>` called `searchHitParser` so that it knows how to deserialize the *inner_hits* section of a hit into a list of nested documents (`NestedDoc`).  
Feel free to adapt the field names/types to match your own mapping.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.ParseField;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.object.ObjectMapper.NestedType;
import org.elasticsearch.search.fetch.innerhits.InnerHitsContext;
import org.elasticsearch.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.xcontent.json.JsonXContent;
import org.elasticsearch.core.internal.io.stream.Writeable.Reader;
import org.elasticsearch.common.util.set.Sets;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Define the POJO(s) that represent the nested document structure.
//     These classes must implement Writeable + ToString/equals/etc.
//
public static class NestedDoc implements Writeable {
    private String title;
    private int   score;

    public NestedDoc() { }

    @Override
    public void writeTo(StreamOutput out) throws IOException {
        out.writeString(title);
        out.writeInt(score);
    }

    public static final Reader<NestedDoc> PARSER =
            new ReadableStreamer<>(in -> {
                NestedDoc d = new NestedDoc();
                d.title = in.readString();
                d.score = in.readVInt();          // or readInt()
                return d;
            });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Create the ObjectParser that will build ParsedDocument instances,
//     including the logic for reading the “inner_hits” array.
//
// NOTE: Replace <YourRootClass> with whatever root container you’re using
//       – e.g., ParsedDocument, MyCustomContainer, etc.
//

private static final ParseField INNER_HITS_FIELD = new ParseField("inner_hits");

@SuppressWarnings({"unchecked", "rawtypes"})
static ObjectParser<Object, Void> declareInnerHitsParseFields(ObjectParser<Object, Void> parser) {

    // Register a sub-parser for each expected inner hit name:
    // For simplicity we assume all inner hits share the same schema.
    //
    // Example: {"my_inner_hit": [ {...}, ... ]}
    parser.declareArray(
        (o, v) -> o.put(INNER_HITS_FIELD.getPreferredName(), v),
        reader -> {
            List<NestedDoc> docs = Lists.newArrayListWithExpectedSize(reader.size());
            while (reader.hasNext()) {
                XContentParser p = reader.nextTokenAsMap().getValue();
                try {
                    docs.add(NestedDoc.PARSER.parse(p));
                } catch (IOException ex) {
                    throw new RuntimeException(ex);      // handle appropriately
                }
            }
            return docs;
        },
        new ParseField("*")           // wildcard – accept any inner-hit key
    );

    return parser;
}

public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Instantiate & configure the parser
    // -------------------------------------------------------------

    ObjectParser<MyResultHolder, Void> searchHitParser =
            new ObjectParser<>("search_hit_parser", true, MyResultHolder::new);

    // Declare standard fields first (e.g., _id, _score)
    searchHitParser.declareString(MyResultHolder::setId, new ParseField("_id"));
    searchHitParser.declareFloat(MyResultHolder::setScore, new ParseField("_score"));

    // Now add the custom inner-hits handling
    declareInnerHitsParseFields(searchHitParser);

    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Use the parser on actual content
    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    byte[] jsonBytes = /* fetch from ES */;
    XContentParser xcp = JsonXContent.createParser(JsonXContent.JSON_CONTENT_TYPE, jsonBytes);
    MyResultHolder holder = searchHitParser.apply(xcp, null);

    System.out.println(holder.toJson());   // inspect parsed data
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Define POJOs** | Represent the shape of the nested documents you’ll receive under `inner_hits`. |
| **Create `declareInnerHitsParseFields()`** | Adds a declaration that tells the parser to look for any field inside `_source["inner_hits"]`, treat its value as an array, and convert each element into a `NestedDoc`. |
| **Instantiate `searchHitParser`** | Builds the top‑level parser that understands both regular hit fields (`_id`, `_score`) and the special `inner_hits` block. |
| **Apply the parser** | Feed raw bytes from Elasticsearch into the parser and obtain a fully populated Java object tree. |

#### Customizing further

* **Multiple inner‑hit types:** Instead of a single wildcard `"*"`, register separate parsers per known inner‑hit key:

  ```java
  parser.declareNamedObjects(..., new ParseField("comments"), CommentPARSER);
  ```

* **Different schemas per inner‑hit:** Provide distinct readers for each key instead of one generic handler.

* **Error handling:** Wrap IOExceptions properly; consider returning optional values or logging failures.

---

If you’d like to see the exact generated JSON after parsing, simply serialize `holder` back to JSON (or override `toString()` accordingly).

